// heartsim tutorial: reading a normal rhythm strip. Original text by the heartsim authors,
// MIT OR Apache-2.0 (see ASSETS-LICENSES.md). Format: see crates/heartsim-core/src/tutorial.rs.
Tutorial(
    format_version: 1,
    id: "02_normal_rhythm",
    title: "Normal rhythm",
    summary: "A five-step routine for reading a rhythm strip: rate, regularity, P waves, PR interval and QRS width, with normal sinus rhythm, bradycardia, tachycardia and sinus arrhythmia.",
    references: [
        "Goldberger AL, Goldberger ZD, Shvilkin A. Goldberger's Clinical Electrocardiography: A Simplified Approach, 9th ed. Elsevier 2017 (ch. 1-3, 13).",
        "Kligfield P et al. Recommendations for the standardization and interpretation of the electrocardiogram, part I. Circulation 2007;115:1306-1324.",
        "Surawicz B et al. Recommendations for the standardization and interpretation of the electrocardiogram, part III: intraventricular conduction disturbances. Circulation 2009;119:e235-e240.",
        "Burns E, Buttner R. Life in the Fast Lane ECG Library: sinus rhythm, sinus bradycardia, sinus tachycardia, sinus arrhythmia. litfl.com/ecg-library.",
    ],
    pages: [
        Page(
            title: "Normal sinus rhythm",
            markdown: r#"
Every heartbeat should start in the **sinoatrial (SA) node**. When it does, the strip shows a
fixed pattern, repeated again and again:

1. a small rounded **P wave** (the atria contract),
2. a short flat stretch while the impulse crosses the AV node,
3. a narrow, tall **QRS complex** (the ventricles contract),
4. a rounded **T wave** (the ventricles recover).

A sinus P wave is **upright in leads I and II** and **inverted in aVR**, because the impulse
travels from the SA node, at the top right of the atria, down and to the left.

The simulator is running a healthy heart at 70 beats per minute. Watch the strip in lead II and
name each wave as it passes.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
                Ui(Resume),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which wave is produced when the ventricles depolarise?",
                choices: ["The P wave", "The QRS complex", "The T wave", "The PR interval"],
                answer: 1,
                explanation: "The QRS complex is ventricular depolarisation. The P wave is atrial depolarisation, the T wave is ventricular repolarisation, and the PR interval is a time, not a wave.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Step 1: the rate",
            markdown: r#"
The first question about any strip is *how fast?* Count the QRS complexes in a 6-second stretch
and multiply by 10, or divide 300 by the number of large (5 mm) boxes between two R waves.

The normal adult resting rate is **60 to 100 beats per minute**.

The simulator first sets the sinus rate to **50 bpm** and, six seconds later, to **90 bpm**.
Neither is abnormal: a slow normal rate is common in fit people, and a faster one after a coffee
or a flight of stairs. Watch how the R–R distance changes while every beat keeps its shape.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Sim(SetGlobalSinusRate(bpm: 50.0)),
                Ui(WaitForEvent(ElapsedMs(6000.0))),
                Sim(SetGlobalSinusRate(bpm: 90.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "The R waves are 4 large boxes (0.8 s) apart. What is the rate?",
                choices: ["About 60 bpm", "About 75 bpm", "About 100 bpm", "About 150 bpm"],
                answer: 1,
                explanation: "300 divided by 4 large boxes is 75 beats per minute. Check: 0.8 s per beat means 60 / 0.8 = 75 beats in a minute.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "A slow sinus rhythm",
            markdown: r#"
A sinus rhythm slower than **60 bpm** is **sinus bradycardia**. Everything else is normal: a P
wave before every QRS, the same PR interval, a narrow QRS. Only the pauses between beats are
longer.

It is normal in athletes and during sleep, and it is caused by drugs such as beta-blockers,
by an underactive thyroid, and by raised vagal tone. It matters when it causes symptoms such as
dizziness or fainting.

This scenario runs at 45 bpm. Find the P wave in front of every QRS.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_brady_45")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "A fit 25-year-old marathon runner has a regular rhythm at 48 bpm, a P wave before every QRS and no symptoms. What is the best description?",
                choices: [
                    "Sinus bradycardia, a normal finding for an athlete",
                    "Complete heart block",
                    "Junctional escape rhythm",
                    "Sinus arrest",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "A P wave before every QRS with a constant PR interval means every beat comes from the sinus node. At 48 bpm it is sinus bradycardia, which is normal when the person is fit and well.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "A fast sinus rhythm",
            markdown: r#"
A sinus rhythm faster than **100 bpm** is **sinus tachycardia**. The beats still start in the SA
node, so the P waves look the same; they just crowd closer to the preceding T wave.

Sinus tachycardia is an *answer*, not a disease. Look for the question: fever, pain, anxiety,
anaemia, blood loss, low oxygen, an overactive thyroid, a pulmonary embolism, or a stimulant.

At rest an adult sinus rate rarely goes far above **150 bpm**. A rate stuck at exactly 150 should
make you think of another rhythm, which later tutorials will show.

Here the sinus rate is 130 bpm.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_tachy_130")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "What is the right first response to sinus tachycardia?",
                choices: [
                    "Look for and treat the cause",
                    "Give a drug to slow the sinus node",
                    "Deliver a shock",
                    "Ignore it, it is always harmless",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "The sinus node is speeding up for a reason. Treating the fever, pain, bleeding or hypoxia settles the rate; slowing the rate directly can be harmful when the body needs the extra output.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Step 2: regularity",
            markdown: r#"
Next, are the R–R intervals even? In a healthy young person they are **not quite**: the sinus
rate speeds up as you breathe in and slows as you breathe out. This is **respiratory sinus
arrhythmia**, a sign of a healthy, well-regulated heart.

How to tell it from a real arrhythmia:

* the P waves all look the same,
* the PR interval is constant,
* the rate changes *gradually*, in step with breathing,
* it fades with age and when the person holds their breath.

The scenario shows the rate waxing and waning twelve times a minute.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_arrhythmia")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which finding makes an irregular rhythm a normal sinus arrhythmia?",
                choices: [
                    "Identical P waves and a gradual change of rate with breathing",
                    "No P waves at all",
                    "Wide QRS complexes appearing early",
                    "A fixed 3:1 relationship between P waves and QRS",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Identical P waves with a constant PR interval show a single sinus source. A gradual speeding and slowing with breathing is respiratory sinus arrhythmia. The other choices describe atrial fibrillation, ventricular ectopics and AV block.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Steps 3 to 5: P, PR and QRS",
            markdown: r#"
Three more checks complete the routine.

* **P wave:** is there one before every QRS, and one QRS after every P?
* **PR interval:** from the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS. Normal is
  **0.12 to 0.20 s** (3 to 5 small boxes, 120 to 200 ms).
* **QRS width:** normally **under about 0.10 to 0.11 s**. A QRS of **0.12 s or more** points to a
  bundle-branch pattern, a ventricular beat, or pacing.

The simulator is back at a normal 60 bpm. Open the 12-lead view and check that every beat passes
all five tests.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Sim(SetGlobalSinusRate(bpm: 60.0)),
                Ui(FocusPanel(TwelveLead)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which strip passes all five tests: rate, regularity, P waves, PR interval, QRS width?",
                choices: [
                    "72 bpm, regular, a P before each QRS, PR 160 ms, QRS 90 ms",
                    "72 bpm, regular, a P before each QRS, PR 280 ms, QRS 90 ms",
                    "72 bpm, regular, no visible P waves, QRS 90 ms",
                    "72 bpm, regular, a P before each QRS, PR 160 ms, QRS 150 ms",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Only the first strip is normal. A PR of 280 ms is first-degree AV block, a missing P wave means the rhythm is not sinus, and a QRS of 150 ms is a wide-complex rhythm.",
            )),
        ),
    ],
)
